# Line-ending and binary policy.
#
# A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites text files on the way to
# the working tree.  For most sources that is harmless, but a few file kinds
# here are broken by it, so pin them explicitly.

# Binary fixtures: BER blobs and serialised nvstate.  A CRLF rewrite silently
# corrupts these, and the tests that read them fail in confusing ways.
*.ber   binary
*.bin   binary

# `patch` matches context lines literally.  CRLF in a patch, or in the file the
# patch targets, makes every hunk fail -- and asn1/*.patch is applied on every
# build that regenerates the codec.
*.patch text eol=lf

# The codec generator runs under bash (MSYS2 on Windows).  A CRLF shebang line
# is not recognised.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Golden test output.  The comparison uses `cmake -E compare_files --ignore-eol`
# so either ending passes, but keeping LF keeps diffs quiet across platforms.
*.ok    text eol=lf
*.err   text eol=lf
*.pem   text eol=lf
